### What is LinkedBlockingQueue?

LinkedBlockingQueue is part of `java.util.concurrent` and is the most commonly used blocking queue in Java. It is designed for producer-consumer scenarios where one or more threads produce items and one or more threads consume them.

The queue provides three types of methods for each operation:

| Operation | Throws exception | Returns special value | Blocks | Times out |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Insert | add(e) – ISE if full | offer(e) – false if full | put(e) – waits | offer(e, time, unit) |
| Remove | remove() – NSEE if empty | poll() – null if empty | take() – waits | poll(time, unit) |
| Examine | element() – NSEE if empty | peek() – null if empty | N/A | N/A |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransactionQueueDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        // Bounded queue: max 100 pending transactions
        BlockingQueue<String> transactionQueue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(100);

        // Producer thread
        Thread producer = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                transactionQueue.put("TXN-001: Deposit 50000 to SAV2001");
                transactionQueue.put("TXN-002: Withdraw 10000 from CUR3001");
                transactionQueue.put("TXN-003: Transfer 25000 SAV2001->CUR3001");
                System.out.println("Producer: 3 transactions queued");
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        });

        // Consumer thread
        Thread consumer = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
                    String txn = transactionQueue.take(); // blocks if empty
                    System.out.println("Consumer processing: " + txn);
                    Thread.sleep(100); // simulate processing time
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        });

        producer.start();
        consumer.start();
        producer.join();
        consumer.join();
    }
}
```

```text
Producer: 3 transactions queued
Consumer processing: TXN-001: Deposit 50000 to SAV2001
Consumer processing: TXN-002: Withdraw 10000 from CUR3001
Consumer processing: TXN-003: Transfer 25000 SAV2001->CUR3001
```

### Why is the two-lock design important?

LinkedBlockingQueue uses two separate `ReentrantLock` instances: `putLock` for the tail and `takeLock` for the head. This means a producer adding to the tail and a consumer removing from the head can work simultaneously without blocking each other.

In contrast, `ArrayBlockingQueue` uses a single lock for both operations. Under high contention (many producers and consumers), LinkedBlockingQueue's two-lock design provides significantly higher throughput.

```java
// LinkedBlockingQueue internal (simplified)
private final ReentrantLock putLock = new ReentrantLock();
private final ReentrantLock takeLock = new ReentrantLock();
private final Condition notEmpty = takeLock.newCondition();
private final Condition notFull = putLock.newCondition();
private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(); // lock-free size

// put() acquires putLock only
// take() acquires takeLock only
// They can execute concurrently!
```

### Why should you always use bounded capacity?

The default constructor `new LinkedBlockingQueue<>()` creates an unbounded queue with capacity `Integer.MAX_VALUE`. This is dangerous because `put()` never blocks – the queue grows without limit. If producers are faster than consumers, the queue consumes all available heap memory and the application crashes with OutOfMemoryError.

`Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n)` internally uses an unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue. In a banking system processing thousands of transactions per second, this means task submissions never fail – they pile up silently until the JVM runs out of memory.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class BoundedVsUnboundedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // DANGEROUS – unbounded queue
        // BlockingQueue<String> unbounded = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();
        // If producer submits 10M tasks and consumer processes 1K/sec,
        // queue grows to 10M elements -> OOM!

        // SAFE – bounded queue with back-pressure
        BlockingQueue<String> bounded = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(1000);
        // When queue reaches 1000, put() blocks the producer
        // This is back-pressure: producer slows down to match consumer speed

        // SAFE – ThreadPoolExecutor with bounded queue
        ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
            4,                            // core threads
            8,                            // max threads
            60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,         // keep-alive
            new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(500) // bounded!
        );
        // When queue is full and max threads are busy,
        // RejectedExecutionHandler is invoked (default: AbortPolicy -> throws)
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

### How is LinkedBlockingQueue different from ArrayBlockingQueue?

| Feature | LinkedBlockingQueue | ArrayBlockingQueue |
|---|---|---|
| Backing structure | Linked nodes | Fixed-size array |
| Locks | Two (put + take) | One (shared) |
| Bounded/Unbounded | Both | Always bounded |
| Fairness option | No | Yes (fair=true) |
| Memory allocation | Per-node (GC pressure) | Pre-allocated array |
| Best for | High throughput | Predictable latency |

```java
// ArrayBlockingQueue – always bounded, single lock, optional fairness
BlockingQueue<String> abq = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1000);
// fair=true: longest-waiting thread gets served first (prevents starvation)
BlockingQueue<String> fairAbq = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1000, true);

// LinkedBlockingQueue – two locks, higher throughput
BlockingQueue<String> lbq = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(1000);
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: Using unbounded queue in production

```java
// WRONG – unbounded, risks OOM
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
// Internally uses new LinkedBlockingQueue<>() – no capacity limit!

// CORRECT – explicit bounded queue
ThreadPoolExecutor pool = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
    4, 4, 0, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
    new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(1000)
);
```

#### Mistake 2: Ignoring InterruptedException

```java
// WRONG – swallowing interrupt
try {
    String txn = queue.take();
} catch (InterruptedException e) {
    // do nothing – BAD! Thread interrupt flag is lost
}

// CORRECT – restore interrupt flag
try {
    String txn = queue.take();
} catch (InterruptedException e) {
    Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); // restore flag
    return;
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Memory overhead.** Each element in LinkedBlockingQueue is wrapped in a Node object (~40 bytes overhead). For millions of queued items, this matters. ArrayBlockingQueue has no per-element overhead because it uses a pre-allocated array.

- **Draining.** `drainTo(collection, maxElements)` atomically removes up to maxElements and adds them to the target collection. This is more efficient than polling in a loop because it acquires the lock once.

- **SynchronousQueue.** A queue with capacity zero. `put()` blocks until a consumer calls `take()`. This is a direct hand-off with no buffering. Used by `Executors.newCachedThreadPool()`.

- **Poison pill pattern.** To signal consumers to stop, put a special "poison pill" object in the queue. When the consumer takes the poison pill, it exits its processing loop.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between put() and offer()?" – put blocks, offer returns false.
- "Why is unbounded queue dangerous?" – producers never slow down, queue grows until OOM.
- "How is LinkedBlockingQueue different from ArrayBlockingQueue?" – two locks vs one, linked nodes vs array.
- "What does Executors.newFixedThreadPool use internally?" – unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue (dangerous).
